### Common numeric sampler

Use one recorder throughout every runtime item, at minimum at `before`,
`after lab.load`, `after lab.start`, `ready`, and `after lab.stop`. During the
soak sample every 60 seconds using UTC timestamps and elapsed monotonic seconds.
Record raw source output as well as parsed columns.

| Required measurement | Concrete source and fields |
|---|---|
| Boot time | Monotonic duration in milliseconds from accepted `lab.start` request to the last required node's real usable console prompt. Also retain each node's individual duration, `lab.start` started/failed arrays, and first-prompt text. VM/GUI readiness is separately timed from launcher invocation to `GET /` returning a status below 500. |
| Packet loss | For every traffic direction, retain the last complete IOS/VPCS success summary and parse transmitted, received, lost, and loss percentage. Record cold/warm tests separately. Soak CSV contains those four fields for each interval plus cumulative totals; never infer loss from capture packet count. |
| Load | Guest `/proc/loadavg` (1/5/15 minute fields and runnable/total tasks) plus `uptime`; host `top -l 1 -s 0` load averages. |
| Per-node RSS | Discover node PIDs from the supervisor's returned node/process identity where available and corroborate with full command lines. For each actual IOL/VPCS child record PID, node id/name, RSS KiB from `/proc/<pid>/status` (`VmRSS`) and `ps -o pid,ppid,rss,vsz,etime,command`. Do not aggregate four nodes into one number. |
| Guest memory | `free -b`, selected `/proc/meminfo` fields (`MemTotal`, `MemAvailable`, `MemFree`, `Buffers`, `Cached`, `SwapTotal`, `SwapFree`), and guest OOM/kernel evidence (`journalctl -k` since run start). Record bytes and compute used/available percentage in the summary. |
| Host memory pressure | Raw `top -l 1 -s 0` PhysMem and load lines, `memory_pressure -Q`, `sysctl vm.swapusage`, and the Lima/VZ process RSS from `ps`. Parse wired, active, compressed, unused, swap used, and the tool's free/pressure percentage where present. The minimum, maximum, and final values belong in `summary.json`. |
| Capture health | Bytes, complete blocks, SHB/IDB count, enhanced/simple packet count, first/last packet timestamp when present, SHA-256, collector connection status, and the elapsed time of the last received packet. Store validation results at the start, every 30 minutes, and the end. |

The record must include the exact numeric value even when a bar only says
â€œzeroâ€ or â€œnon-zero.â€ Missing, unparsable, or stale samples are failures of the
qualification harness, not values to report as zero.

